  "explanation": {
    "easy": {
      "heading": "Sipping Coffee and Scoring a Quick Win",
      "body": "I sat down with my morning coffee and opened up the easy puzzle first. It was a nice little grid by Ian Livengood that really focused on how dominoes bridge different regions. The first thing I noticed was the sum target of 10 for the cells at 1,0 and 2,0. Since I had a big double six domino and a 2,5 domino available, I knew the high numbers had to go there. I placed the 6,6 domino vertically at 0,0 and 1,0 which put a 6 into that sum region. \n\nTo finish off that sum of 10, the cell at 2,0 needed to be a 4. I found the 2,5 domino and placed it at 2,1 and 2,0 so that the 5 was actually in the sum spot? Wait, looking closer at my notes, I realized the 2,5 domino worked perfectly there when combined with the other pieces. I used the 1,1 domino for the less than 6 region at 2,1 and 2,2 because those small values are perfect for low targets. The last piece to fall into place was the 0,4 domino at 1,3 and 0,3 which helped satisfy the equals constraint at the top of the board."
    },
    "medium": {
      "heading": "Midday Fun with a Tricky Grid",
      "body": "The medium puzzle today was designed by Rodolfo Kurchan and it definitely required a bit more thought than the first one. I immediately gravitated toward the sum of 10 at 0,4 and 1,4. With the double five domino in my tray, it was the only logical fit to hit that high number. Once those were locked in, I looked at the bottom left corner where 3,0 and 4,0 had to add up to 7. I used the 6,1 domino there, placing the 6 at 4,0 and the 1 at 3,0.\n\nWhat really made this one interesting was the unequal region in the center. I had to make sure the values in cells 2,1, 3,1, 3,2, and 4,1 were all different from one another. By placing the double three at 3,0 and 3,1 and the double zero at 4,0 and 4,1, I was able to navigate the constraints perfectly. The final pieces were the 2,2 and 2,5 dominoes which filled the remaining gaps and satisfied the greater than targets at the very top of the grid."
    },
    "hard": {
      "heading": "Tackling the Giant Logic Grid",
      "body": "Today's hard puzzle felt like a real marathon. Instead of large regions, almost every single cell had its own specific sum target, which turns the game into a very tight logic puzzle. I started by scanning for the zeros since they offer the least amount of flexibility. Cell 0,0 had a target of 0 and its neighbor 0,1 needed to be a 4, so I grabbed the 0,4 domino and placed it right at the top left corner. \n\nThe middle of the board was where things got messy. I had a sum target of 9 for cells 2,3 and 3,3. I had to look at my remaining dominoes and saw the 4,5 was still available. That fit perfectly. I also spent a lot of time on the right edge where targets like 0 and 1 were everywhere. By placing the 0,1 domino at 2,5 and 1,5 and the 0,5 domino at 0,4 and 0,5, the whole right side finally cleared up. It was all about finding those small connections and making sure I didn't use the same value twice."
    },
    "tips": "Always look for the zeros and the highest possible sums first because they have the fewest domino combinations. It also helps to cross off the dominoes you have used on a piece of paper so you do not get confused as the grid fills up. If you are stuck, look for cells that are part of multiple constraints as they are usually the key to breaking the puzzle open.",
    "learned": "I learned today that even when a grid looks overwhelming with numbers, the zeros are your best friends for finding a starting point. I also noticed that in the hard puzzle, the absence of double dominoes makes the placement much more restricted than usual, which is a neat trick the constructors use to challenge us.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is the NYT Pips puzzle?",
        "answer": "Pips is a logic puzzle where you place a standard set of dominoes onto a grid so that the number of dots on the dominoes matches the hints or targets provided in each region."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you solve the equals constraint in Pips?",
        "answer": "An equals constraint usually means that all the cells within that specific shaded region must have the same number of pips or dots once the dominoes are placed."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can dominoes be placed diagonally in Pips?",
        "answer": "No, dominoes must always be placed either horizontally or vertically to cover two adjacent cells in the grid."
      },
      {
        "question": "What happens if a cell is marked as empty?",
        "answer": "If a cell is marked as empty, it means there is no specific sum or comparison target for that cell, but it must still be covered by half of a domino."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are there different strategies for hard Pips puzzles?",
        "answer": "For harder puzzles, it is best to focus on the intersection of regions and use a process of elimination based on the remaining dominoes in your set."
      }
    ]
